{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And he shall take off his garments</b>. This is not an obligation but rather proper conduct — that he not soil the garments in which he serves during the removal of the ashes. <i>He who cooked a pot for his master should not pour a cup for his master with the same pot.</i> Therefore it says, <b>\"and he shall put on other garments\"</b> — lesser than they; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 6:4). And the intention of our Sages in this matter is to say that the removal of the ashes requires priestly vestments, and <b>\"other garments\"</b> are not non-sacred garments. But I do not know from where the Rav said that it is not an obligation, for it appears that it is a commandment upon the priest — that the garments in which he performs the offerings and also the lifting of the ashes shall be clean garments; he shall not serve in those in which he took out the ashes. And the commandment follows the discipline of a slave toward his master, and therefore the priests would have the finer garments for the sacred service and the lesser ones for the removal of the ashes. And all this is according to the reasoning that the Rav wrote. But there are among our Sages there in tractate Yoma (Yoma 23) who say that the removal of the ashes does not require priestly vestments, and they expound <b>\"and he shall put on other garments\"</b> as non-sacred garments. And the plain sense of Scripture is accordingly that He commands him not to soil the linen garments, the sacred garments, during the removal of the ashes, but rather he shall put on non-sacred garments."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "The proverb about the pot and cup appears in b. Shabbat 114a and is cited by Rashi ad loc.; Ramban quotes it in Hebrew as a well-known dictum."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:50:53.664476803Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}